diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage between the 172.16.20.x and 172.16.10.x networks. From a client on 172.16.20.x (laptop0, 172.16.20.10) I can reach multiple 172.16.10.x hosts, including the specifically mentioned PC4 (172.16.10.4), with 0% loss and ~1.2 ms RTT. I also reached PC5 (172.16.10.5) and the 172.16.10.x laptops successfully. Laptop0 is correctly configured with default gateway 172.16.20.1, and router0 has subinterfaces eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) and eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) up and routing. The campus switch stack (mswitch1) shows VLAN bridges br_vlan20 and br_vlan10 up, with trunking toward router0 and access ports toward switch1, so L2/VLAN segmentation is healthy.

Mechanism: Inter-VLAN traffic from 172.16.20.0/24 to 172.16.10.0/24 transits router0 and is presently forwarding as expected; verified by successful ICMP from the affected subnet to multiple 172.16.10.x endpoints.

Localization: Path laptop0 -> switch1 -> mswitch1 (VLAN20) -> router0 eth0.20 -> router0 eth0.10 -> mswitch1 (VLAN10) -> switch1 -> PC4 is operational.

Root cause: No persistent network fault found; the earlier symptom was likely transient or host/application-specific (e.g., name resolution or print service on the laptop), not a network connectivity issue.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask the user to retry by IP (e.g., ping 172.16.10.4). If issues persist, clear DNS/hosts cache, verify printer/workstation IPs, and check the laptop’s firewall/print spooler, as the routed path is confirmed healthy.
